[s0l0!-header][h1]Using Loops with Arrays[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Many algorithms require accessing every element of an array to check for data, store information, and other tasks. This can be done in a process called [b]traversing the array[/b], which is often implemented with a for loop because the loop control variable naturally corresponds to array indexes.
Consider the following program: [code format="c" codeId="1669"]float purchases[3] = {10.99, 14.25, 90.50};
float total = 0;
int k;

/* total the purchases */
for (k = 0; k < 3; k++) {
  total += purchases[k];
}
   
printf("Purchases total is %6.2f\n", total);
/* Output:  Purchases total is 115.74 */ [/code]
The loop control variable iterates from 0 to one less than the number of elements to match index values.
Loops are also useful for assignment.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1670"]int a[10];
int k;

for (k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
  a[k] = k * 10;
} [/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

